Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Author
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action contemporary American drama; naturalistic faces, practical Midwest and Pacific Northwest locations, grounded lighting.

FADE IN:

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A cramped fifth-floor walk-up overlooks Milwaukee. NICK OLSEN, broad-shouldered, scarred at the chin and left eyebrow, with reddish-brown unruly hair and heavy work boots, carries grocery bags. His younger brother, THE NARRATOR, lean and dark-haired, follows with two bags.

(SOUND: stairwell footsteps, grocery bags rustling, a radiator knocking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Nick was the guy everybody was scared of.

NICK
You got the light ones again, Bro?

NARRATOR
You carry six bags like it’s nothing.

NICK
Somebody’s got to.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Nick approaches a curl bench. KEVIN MCCLEARY, muscular and neat-haired, immediately abandons a barbell for him. The Narrator watches Nick replace it with a heavier bar.

(SOUND: weights clanging, treadmills humming, locker doors slamming)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When Nick walked into the bar or the gym, people moved out of the way.

NICK
Fifty.

KEVIN MCCLEARY
That’s just your warm-up?

NICK
You know it is.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Twelve-year-old NICK stands before furious MRS. ENGEL, an elderly woman in a floral housecoat. A broken kitchen window gapes behind her. Young Narrator and MARK CONNER hide near dented garbage-can lids.

(SOUND: children’s kickball bouncing, glass tinkling underfoot, traffic passing)

MRS. ENGEL
Who kicked that ball through my kitchen window?

NICK
I did.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Mark Conner kicked it. Nick took it, and somehow gave Ma the money a week later.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

MA, OLIVIA OLSEN, a tired older woman with soft brown hair and failing eyesight, sits before a game show. Pill bottles stand in a neat line on the kitchen counter.

(SOUND: game-show applause on television, pill bottles clicking, clock ticking)

NICK
What’s Ma doing in bed in the middle of the day?

NARRATOR
I don’t know.

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That’s how old people go downhill.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had a job at the metal factory, a burnt-orange Trans Am, and an opinion about everything.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

The Narrator parks his bicycle outside a modest pizza shop. SIONNA MCALLISTER stumbles on the sidewalk and brushes against him. She is slender, pale, with short brown hair, light-blue eyes, a black V-neck shirt, jeans, blue sneakers, and a fish-symbol necklace.

(SOUND: bicycle chain ticking, city traffic, a pizzeria door chime)

NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

NARRATOR
I work here. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

NARRATOR
Sionna? That’s different.

SIONNA
My parents named me after an Irish goddess.

NARRATOR
You want some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks. I don’t eat pizza. But if you want coffee sometime, you can call me.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Her eyes were light blue, like the sky before the sun comes up.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma searches the counter, her hands trembling among the pill bottles. The Narrator enters, still carrying his pizza-shop jacket.

(SOUND: refrigerator motor buzzing, television murmuring from the living room)

MA
I can’t find my pain pills. Did we go to church today?

NARRATOR
Your pills are right here, Ma.

MA
Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

NARRATOR
We haven’t been to church in years.

MA
Oh. Right. I see them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Her eyes went glassy and empty. I knew I had to tell Nick.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Nick sits beside the Narrator at a crowded bar, football glowing silently on a television. Nick’s scarred face tightens as he listens.

(SOUND: Packers game crowd roar, pool balls cracking, rock music from a jukebox)

NARRATOR
Ma got confused. She couldn’t find her pills.

NICK
Why don’t you take her to the store or something?

NARRATOR
I just needed to get away for a while.

NICK
Do something before she turns into some demented old fart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sometimes I really hated Nick inside.

INT. MILWAUKEE PUBLIC LIBRARY - NIGHT

The Narrator enters a grand old library: wood floors, hanging lamps, long tables, endless shelves. LIBRARIAN ADA BELL, gray-haired and composed, works behind the desk.

(SOUND: pages turning, distant footsteps on wood, a radiator hissing)

NARRATOR
How do I take a book home?

LIBRARIAN ADA BELL
Do you have a library card?

NARRATOR
No. How do I get one?

LIBRARIAN ADA BELL
Fill this out. I’ll give you a temporary card.

The Narrator finds WUTHERING HEIGHTS and opens it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who needed school when all this was here for free?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Sionna sits at a small back table in a loose green hooded top, backpack at her feet. The Narrator approaches with Wuthering Heights in hand.

(SOUND: espresso machine steaming, cups clinking, low student chatter)

SIONNA
What are you reading?

NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. It’s about a guy and girl who grow apart but are really in love.

SIONNA
The moors are like the people. Wild, unpredictable, infertile.

NARRATOR
You read it?

SIONNA
Of course.

SIONNA
Heathcliff’s love never changes. In the end, that kind of love destroys him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Something inside me was coming awake.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

The Narrator struggles on a purple mat in an awkward downward dog. Sionna moves easily nearby, her blue eyes calm.

(SOUND: slow breathing, fabric stretching, a faint floorboard creak)

SIONNA
Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what’s going on on your mat.

NARRATOR
My practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That’s why they call it practice.

The Narrator lies still in corpse pose, breathing deeply.

(MUSIC: soft sustained singing bowl fading into silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For once, I didn’t feel violent, mad, or scared. I just felt calm.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma sits small beneath an afghan. Nick looms over the Narrator, fists clenching. The blue weekly pill organizer sits on the table.

(SOUND: game-show theme from television, Nick’s boots pacing, a wall clock ticking)

NICK
Ma missed her doctor’s appointment yesterday, you ass.

NARRATOR
I forgot. I was with someone.

NICK
A girl is worth all this?

NARRATOR
I said I was sorry.

NICK
Try looking at your priorities, Bro.

Nick slams the apartment door.

(SOUND: door slamming, Ma’s sharp intake of breath)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had started to see Nick differently. Not as a warrior. Just as a person.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - NIGHT

Ma lies in a hospital bed, pale but awake. The Narrator dozes in a chair. Sionna arrives with sandwiches and sits beside him.

(SOUND: heart monitor beeping, rolling carts, distant intercom announcement)

SIONNA
Your mother is going to be okay tonight.

NARRATOR
I’m trying to do everything right.

SIONNA
You don’t have to hold the whole world alone.

The Narrator falls asleep.

EXT. MEDIEVAL BATTLEFIELD - NIGHT

The Narrator, now in mud-streaked tunic and leather boots, stands amid smoke and fallen soldiers. Nick, armored and enormous, rushes through the chaos with a knife.

(SOUND: swords striking, men shouting, wind tearing across the field)

NARRATOR
Nick! Thank God you’re here!

NICK
That you are, little brother.

Nick drives the knife toward him.

(SOUND: blade plunging, a thunderous heartbeat, sudden silence)

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL - NIGHT

The Narrator jolts awake, gasping. Ma sleeps beside the monitor.

(SOUND: heart monitor returning to its steady beep, rain tapping the window)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick had killed me in the dream. I told myself it was only a dream.

INT. JOE'S BAR AND GRILL - NIGHT

Nick, BOB, and SAMMY drink near the pool tables. The Narrator sits apart, uncomfortable. LINDSEY, Nick’s blonde ex-girlfriend, stands with DAVID, dark-haired and wary.

(SOUND: AC/DC on jukebox, beer bottles clinking, pool balls cracking)

NICK
He’s got the perfect girlfriend. She has him wrapped.

NARRATOR
You three wouldn’t recognize class and intelligence if it bit your ass.

Nick spots Lindsey and rises.

NICK
Who’s that asshole with Lindsey?

NARRATOR
You broke up with her. Leave it alone.

DAVID
You got a problem with me?

NICK
You’re in my face like a little bitch.

NARRATOR steps between them.

NARRATOR
No one cares about your ego, Nick!

Nick’s fist strikes the Narrator across the face.

(SOUND: fist connecting, Lindsey crying out, a cue stick dropping)

JOE and BOUNCER RAY restrain Nick as the Narrator runs from the bar.

(SOUND: bodies struggling, a chair scraping, heavy footsteps fleeing)

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Sionna’s old duplex glows warmly. She holds the Narrator on a green suede sofa, his cheek reddening from Nick’s blow.

(SOUND: rain on the windows, a kettle softly rattling, the cat purring nearby)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

NARRATOR
He does care about me. I can’t explain Nick completely.

SIONNA
No one who cares about you does that.

NARRATOR
I’m afraid of being alone.

SIONNA
You are becoming a bravely compassionate being. You can’t let him intimidate you.

NARRATOR
What is my purpose?

SIONNA
Find the true self within you, and honor it.

They kiss.

(MUSIC: warm acoustic guitar, quiet and intimate)

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Months later. Nick’s Werner Street apartment is littered with takeout containers, clothes, and unopened mail. Nick, unshaven and thinner, opens the chained door a crack.

(SOUND: chain lock rattling, microwave humming, traffic below)

NICK
What do you want?

NARRATOR
Ma is worried about you.

NICK
You ruined my life. Everybody says you stopped me at Joe’s.

NARRATOR
I didn’t start that story.

NICK
You’re some hot-shot college kid now.

NARRATOR
I’m working my ass off and finding out how much I don’t know.

Nick looks away.

NICK
I’ll see Ma tomorrow. Don’t interrupt me in public again.

NARRATOR
Will you come see her?

NICK
Yeah.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Sionna serves peach pie to Ma. Nick enters in his brown suede jacket, wild reddish-brown hair, work boots, and guarded posture.

(SOUND: forks scraping plates, television detective show, elevator cables groaning outside)

MA
Nick! I thought we would never see you again.

SIONNA
I’m Sionna. Nice to meet you.

NICK
Likewise.

NICK
I hear you’re a university lady.

SIONNA
I hope to teach. English, philosophy, whatever lets people think.

NICK
It’s tough out there. Especially for women.

SIONNA
You can’t keep a good woman down.

NICK
Women ought to stay home and take care of kids.

SIONNA
Many women have no choice. Education gives people more ways to live.

Ma watches Sionna with admiration. Nick’s jaw tightens.

NICK
Take care of Ma.

He grips the Narrator’s shirt briefly and leaves.

(SOUND: apartment door clicking shut, a cigarette lighter sparking in the hall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick came around more after that. Too much more. I should have questioned it.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment is silent. Ma’s chair is empty; her afghan is folded. The answering machine’s red light blinks.

(SOUND: answering machine beep, refrigerator hum, distant siren)

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olsen, this is Officer Mike Dupuis. We need to speak with you regarding your family.

NARRATOR presses another message.

NURSE OWENS (V.O.)
This is Patricia Owens at Frodo Hospital, with an update on your family members.

NARRATOR
Oh God.

EXT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

The Narrator pedals his bicycle hard through gray streets toward an old stone station.

(SOUND: bicycle tires hissing on wet pavement, traffic horns, his ragged breathing)

INT. MILWAUKEE POLICE STATION - DAY

OFFICER DUPUIS, broad and gray-haired, sits across from the Narrator in a small interview room.

(SOUND: fluorescent lights buzzing, phones ringing beyond the door)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Last night, your brother and mother were hit at Werner and Lowe.

NARRATOR
Is Ma okay?

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your mother passed away at the scene.

NARRATOR grips a paper cup of water until it buckles.

(SOUND: paper cup crumpling, a far-off door closing)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Your brother is in serious condition.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick killed her. He fucking killed my mom.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

The Narrator collapses beside Ma’s empty chair. Sionna enters and kneels beside him.

(SOUND: the apartment clock ticking, muffled city traffic, the Narrator sobbing)

NARRATOR
Die, you fucking jackass. Die.

SIONNA
Let it out. We’ll figure it out.

NARRATOR
One day she’s talking to me. The next day I’m putting her in a box.

SIONNA holds him as he shakes.

(MUSIC: low mournful cello under the ticking clock)

EXT. ST. MARY'S CEMETERY - DAY

Ma’s maple-colored casket rests above an open grave. The Narrator drops a handful of dirt. Sionna stands beside him in a black coat.

(SOUND: dirt striking the casket lid, wind through bare branches, distant church bell)

FATHER JOSEPH
Olivia kept the faith through hardship and love.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was the only son at her funeral. The oldest one could not attend his own mother’s funeral.

INT. FRODO HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Nick lies battered beneath white sheets. His face is bruised green and purple; an oxygen tube runs beneath his nose, and a chest tube drains beside him. The Narrator stands at the foot of the bed.

(SOUND: ventilator hiss, monitor alarms, IV pump clicking)

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man.

NARRATOR
They took care of it. The mass was nice.

NICK
I didn’t mean to hurt Ma.

NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

Nick’s face hardens, then collapses under tears.

(SOUND: monitor beeping faster, Nick’s unsteady breath)

NARRATOR
Think about it.

The Narrator turns and walks out.

INT. SIONNA'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months later. Sionna sits in a rocking chair, a healed scar visible near her left collarbone. She stares at the floor, drained and distant. The Narrator sits opposite.

(SOUND: rain against glass, rocking chair creaking, cat paws crossing the hall)

NARRATOR
You know I love you, right?

SIONNA
I can’t feel me. I can’t feel myself anymore.

NARRATOR
You’re safe. I’ll protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is doubt, I can’t go on with you.

NARRATOR
Stay. We can work it out.

SIONNA
I need to go to my parents for a while.

The Narrator’s face folds. Sionna rises and disappears down the hallway.

(MUSIC: a single piano figure, stopping unfinished)

EXT. SEATTLE WATERFRONT - DAY

Seven years later. The Narrator, now older, sturdier, neatly dressed, walks through gray Seattle rain. His hair is shorter; his face carries exhaustion and discipline. A sign reads TONY'S BOY'S PIZZA.

(SOUND: ferry horn, wet tires on pavement, gulls crying over Puget Sound)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I became another person: a man in Washington, with pizza stores, a cat, and a life that looked successful from outside.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles flicker around a small class. The Narrator lies on a mat, eyes closed, breathing steadily.

(SOUND: synchronized breathing, a singing bowl resonating, rain tapping skylights)

INSTRUCTOR MAYA
Think of one thing you can release.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Holding resentment kept me in my own prison.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY - DAY

The Narrator drives a rental car through rolling autumn hills toward Green Bay.

(SOUND: tires on highway, turn signal clicking, wind over the car roof)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had to see Nick. Not to save him. To release myself.

INT. GREEN BAY CORRECTIONAL INSTITUTE - DAY

Behind thick plexiglass, Nick sits in an orange prison uniform. He is thinner, graying at the temples, his old scars softened by age. The Narrator picks up a phone.

(SOUND: metal doors clanging, telephones crackling, guards’ footsteps)

NICK
Hi, Bro. Glad you came.

NARRATOR
Hi, Nick.

NICK
It’s not a cakewalk. I might get parole in two years.

NARRATOR
I’m here to tell you something. Please just listen.

NICK
Okay.

NARRATOR
You killed Ma in a drunk-driving accident.

NARRATOR
You held me down my whole life so you could have the bigger life.

NARRATOR
You tried to kill the love of my life. You drove her away.

NICK lowers his eyes.

NARRATOR
You’re an asshole, Nick.

(SOUND: phone line humming, a guard’s keys rattling)

NARRATOR
But I forgive you.

NICK looks up, tears forming.

NARRATOR
I forgive you because it sets me free.

NICK
I’m sorry. I’m so sorry.

NICK
You were always more than I was.

NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. We’re expanding.

NICK
That’s amazing. I’m proud of you.

GUARD HARRIS
Time to wrap it up.

NARRATOR
I still love you for being my brother. I want you to get better.

NICK
I love you too, man.

The Narrator hangs up. Nick is led away.

(SOUND: receiver settling into its cradle, metal door locking, footsteps fading)

EXT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

The Narrator joins the boarding line, lighter in posture. He studies his boarding pass.

(SOUND: boarding announcements, rolling luggage, jet engine rumble)

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

The Narrator smiles.

PETER OLSEN
That’s me. President Peter Olsen.

FADE OUT.

THE END.
